From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: Human Language (was The Importance of Reading)

Jeff Fabijanic writes

>Great post Simon! (Hey that's pretty cool - any simple sentence that ends
>with your name ends up looking like the writer intended a particularly
>forceful statement, whether he did or not. I did, of course ;)

Thanks.  Of course, there is no prohibition on appending further bangs to the
right. ;-)

>I've seen people make this distinction before, but I have to wonder, is it
>reasonable to make the assumption that we *aren't* biologically disposed to
>reading and writing? If R&W are such a great advantages, then it seems as
>though any humans with a genetic prediliction to readily understand abstract  
>visual symbol systems (ie, most modern written languages) would have a leg up
>in suceeding in their culture and passing the advantage onto their children.
>If good readers and writers made more than their illiterate counterparts, they
>would generally be able to afford more food, better health care, superior
>shelter and clothing, etc. After a few thousand years of this, might we not
>expect there to exist a population shift towards any genomes that potentiated
>that ability?

All this makes sense in the abstract, but when you look at the facts, you get a different 
picture: by all accounts, written language extends back over no more than the past 10,000 
years, whereas the existence of homo-sapiens as a species is likely many, many times that 
duration.  Furthermore -- and most convincingly, in my opinion -- whereas every human 
population in existence has a spoken language, the alphabet was invented only once in 
human history, by speakers of a Semitic language.  All subsequent alphabets were based on 
this early Semitic alphabet, either directly (like the Roman alphabet I'm using now) or 
by conceptual imitation (like the Korean alphabet).  So there is very little evidence 
that human beings are predisposed to reading and writing.

Further counter-evidence comes from experiments showing that illiterate adult speakers of 
a language (in the experiments I have heard of, Portuguese) have little or no insight 
into the abstract linguistic properties that make alphabetic spelling possible. Unlike 
spoken language, which is acquired at no effort by almost all parents and children, 
reading and writing have to be taught in school, like math and history.

Of course, all this is largely a matter of your perspective.  If you take what I call the 
Marquis de Sade Position -- that anything that we are capable of is natural -- then you 
can view reading and writing as a biological disposition, but the position buys you very 
little scientifically.  I prefer to think in statistical terms, which is why I conclude 
that reading and writing are a recently invented clever trick that some of us are good 
at.  Since it's been around for such a short time, its value to our selfish genes is an 
open question.

-- Simon!


