X-Folks,

Tim May wrote:

    Teach them that guns are _not_ toys and are not to be
    played with.  Apparently children as young as 4 can get
    this message.

If you have guns and kids in the same house, I think Tim's
solution is the only one that makes any sense.  There is no
"security though obscurity" where children are concerned (see
amazing story below).  Educate your children about guns, give
appropriate warnings, and--most important--allow them to satisfy
their curiosity about weapons under your supervision.

I have always had firearms in my house.  My daughter was shown
the (always loaded) guns, allowed to hold them, told what they
were capable of doing and, when she was old enough, allowed to
fire them.  When she was 14, I bought her a .22 rifle.  For the
record; when your parents *make* you play with guns, you loose
your curiosity really quickly.  ("Do I *have* to go shooting?")

AMAZING STORY

I gave a gun to an ex-girlfriend because I didn't like the
neighborhood she was living in.  She was sharing a house with a
single mother with two children (girl 11, boy 9).  Because of
this, she hid the unloaded gun high in a closet, buried deep in a
stack of cloths.  The ammo was hidden somewhere else.  She never
told the mother or the children anything about the gun.

Her precautions notwithstanding, she decided it wasn't a good
idea to keep the gun there, so she asked me to come pick it up.
When I arrived, she went to the closet only to discover that the
gun was gone!  Ditto for the ammunition.  When all this was
mentioned to the mother in the presence of her children, the kids
exhibited guilty-knowledge reactions.  On further questioning, a
most bizarre tale was told.

It seems the children had been "exploring" my ex-girlfriend's
room while she was at work.  They first found the hidden ammo.
As this obviously implied the presence of a gun, the kids
searched until they found it too.

They then told a teenaged neighborhood boy about the gun.  He
asked to borrow it so he could rob a drug dealer he knew in
downtown San Francisco.  The kids agreed to loan it to him if
they could go along with him and get a share of the loot.

When the got to the dealers place the two kids waited outside
while he went in to pull the job.  He apparently, when he
confronted the dealer with the gun, the dealer just laughed it
off.  This unnerved the teen to such an extent, he accidentally
discharged the gun, which put a hole in the dealer's desk.  This
further panicked the would-be robber, who then dropped the gun
and ran out of the building.

After hearing this story, my ex-girlfriend called the cops who,
after being given this account, went and questioned the teen.  He
admitted everything and even gave them the name of the dealer.
The cops went to the dealer's place and asked him if the story
were true.  He not only verified the story, but turned over the
gun to the officers, who then returned it to my ex-girlfriend,
who gave it back to me.  Ain't life strange?


 S a n d y

P.S.  I like the Glock, but I really love the H&Ks with the
      squeeze cocking mechanisms.  The gun points extremely well,
      has very little barrel climb and has great safety features.
      It's only drawback is its cost, but it is a hell of a gun.

