Tim May writes:

>The best safety method with children around is to:
>
>a. Teach them that guns are _not_ toys and are not to be played with.
>Apparently children as young as 4 can get this message.

I grew up with guns (mostly old military rifles) as a constant presence in the house.
My father took several tacks: (1) He removed the handle to the closet door where the
guns were stored, making it very clear that I was simply not even to think about
opening that closet when he was not around.  Of course, what little ammo he kept in the 
house was in a separate place, which was also forbidden.  (2) When he took a gun out of 
the closet to clean or repair it, he would show me how the mechanism worked, how to 
dry-fire it after making certain that the chamber was empty, etc.  (3) He took me to gun 
shows, so I learned that most people who own guns are responsible, middle class citizens 
like him. (I also picked up some lessons in the workings of the free market and had early 
exposure to libertarian ideas there.)  (4) He taught me how to shoot at a very early age 
(around 6, I think), using a BB gun, and again making it clear that I was absolutely 
forbidden to touch the gun when he wasn't around.  (5) When I was old enough (12) or so, 
he took me to the rifle range to learn how to shoot a real rifle.

My experience doubtless reflects that of many other men (and, I hope, women) in this 
country. Learning about gun safety, that guns *are* potentially dangerous, but that we 
can discipline ourselves to use them safely and beneficially, was an integral part of my 
upbringing, and is an essential part of my relationship to my father.  I feel sorry for 
anti-gun people who can't understand this, who were brought up to believe that material 
objects themselves are evil and to be feared.  I also wonder whether these people really 
understand the emotional/ethical depths they are messing around in when they try to take 
guns from other Americans.

-- Simon!







