If you are getting a license to "carry," then concealability will be
an issue. (Yes, concealability is an issue. In fact, an important
issue. Being "made" by cops, by store security folks, etc., is
generally not a good thing in these hoplohysteric times. Last thing
you want to hear is a frightened woman screaming "HE'S GOT A GUN!!")

I've heard good things about the "Firestar" in 9mm, from Star
(imported by Interarms). It feels very good in my hand, and is rather
heavy (35 ounces, for a small gun).

Contrary to what was being reported recently about the 9mm lacking
stopping power, a good 9mm load (which we've discussed several times
here) has a good percentage of "one-shot stops" according to several
studies. The infamous "Miami shootout" that caused the FBI to switch
to the 10mm led to unwarranted conclusions, many believe. 

The .40 S&W, sometimes nicknamed the "short and wimpy," is a 10mm
diameter bullet but in a slightly shorter case. It's effectively a
compromise between the 9mm and the 10mm. Me, I have so far stuck with
the 9mm because the ammo is widely available and has been for 50+
years, it's got pretty good stopping characteristics, and a couple of
my guns are already 9s.

