Date: Mon, 13 Dec 93 12:20:43 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Bear Essentials

Brian Williams remarks:

> "never try to massage a bear."

    On that note I find myself unable to resist telling the rest of the
bear-versus-entomologist story, which I did not at first post because it
seemed so unbelievable.

    You will recall that a hapless, unarmed field naturalist stumbled
upon mother grizzly and two cubs in a place where the trees were too small
to climb.  She charged.  He fended her off briefly with his climbing axe,
but soon she chomped down on his wrist.

    At this point he got down on his knees and started petting the bear
on the head.  He spoke soothingly to her, telling her that she was a nice
bear, explaining that he was only a college professor and not out to hurt
anyone, et cetera, et cetera.

    Presently she let go.

    Then he dropped to prone position, covered the back of his neck with
his collar and tucked his hands under him -- no flesh exposed.  She rolled
him over.  He rolled back to prone.  She rolled him over again, and he
rolled back again.  He waited till the bear had gone, then got up and
-- nope, the bear hadn't gone, she charged back, so he dropped to prone
again.  He then waited a longer time, got up -- no bear -- and ran like
hell.  He escaped with minor injuries -- the bite had broken skin but not
bone.

    These events were related in an upper-division vertebrate zoology class
at San Jose State University, that I took this autumn, by the professor.
The person to whom they happened is a recently-retired faculty member of
the San Jose State biology department.  Our zoology professor heard the
story directly from the victim.  I believe my narrative here is a good
paraphrase of what he said.

    Our professor used the story as an illustration of animal behavior,
pointing out that the issue here was dealing with a perceived threat, not
predation, thus the victim's strategy, of appearing as non-threatening
as possible, was rational (though I am not sure that petting the bear
on the head was optimal, and I certainly don't believe it matters what
you tell the bear in such circumstances -- bears are notoriously
susceptible to flattery and presumably anything will do ... :-) ).  The
drop-and-cover-flesh strategy is good not only for appearing non-threatening,
but even if the bear is attempting to take prey, for grizzlies do not have
shearing teeth with which they can bite big chunks of meat from a large
carcass -- when a bear takes a deer -- or a human -- it has to cache the kill
for a few days, until the body has decomposed sufficiently for it to go
to pieces when the bear starts taking bites.  It's sort of an eat-through
cache.  Thus if drop-and-cover convinces the bear that it has killed you,
what it is likely to do is not to start nibbling right away, but instead
to start covering you with leaves and brush, as a cache.  This has actually
happened from time to time, said our professor.

    This strategy likely would not work with any predator equipped with
shearing teeth.  Such animals include felines, canids and for that matter,
polar bears.

                                 -- Jay Freeman,
                                    who bear-ly believes it himself

